export const frqBank: PrecalcFRQ[] = [{id:`frq-1a`,type:`function-concepts`,calcMode:`calc-required`,unitId:1,topicIds:[`1.7`,`1.10`,`2.7`,`2.8`],context:`The function $f$ is defined for all real numbers by $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^2 - 4}{x - 2}$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = 2x + 5$.`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`(i) Find $\\lim_{x \\to 2} f(x)$, or indicate that the limit does not exist. Show the work that leads to your answer.
(ii) Find $f^{-1}(7)$, or indicate that it is not defined.`,pointName:`A1, A2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) $f(x) = \\frac{(x-2)(x+2)}{x-2} = x + 2$ for $x \\ne 2$. So $\\lim_{x \\to 2} f(x) = 4$.
(ii) $f$ has a hole at $x = 2$ but otherwise equals $x + 2$. To find $f^{-1}(7)$: $x + 2 = 7 \\Rightarrow x = 5$. Since $5 \\ne 2$, $f^{-1}(7) = 5$.`,scoringNotes:`Point A1 earned for limit value 4 with supporting work showing factoring/cancellation. Point A2 earned for $f^{-1}(7) = 5$.`,partialCreditNotes:`A response missing both A1 and A2 is eligible for partial credit if it correctly identifies the hole at $x = 2$ AND attempts the inverse computation, even if the inverse is not solved.`},{letter:`B`,task:`(i) Find $(g \\circ f)(3)$ as a decimal approximation.
(ii) Determine the values of $x$, if any, where $f(x) = g(x)$.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) $f(3) = (9-4)/(3-2) = 5/1 = 5$. So $(g \\circ f)(3) = g(5) = 2(5) + 5 = 15$. As decimal: $15.000$.
(ii) Set $f(x) = g(x)$: $\\frac{x^2-4}{x-2} = 2x + 5$. For $x \\ne 2$: $x + 2 = 2x + 5 \\Rightarrow -x = 3 \\Rightarrow x = -3$. Verify: $f(-3) = (9-4)/(-5) = -1$, $g(-3) = -1$. ✓`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: correct decimal value 15.000 with supporting computation. Point B2: $x = -3$ as the only intersection.`},{letter:`C`,task:`(i) Determine the end behavior of $f$ as $x$ increases without bound. Express your answer using mathematical notation of a limit.
(ii) Determine whether $f$ is one-to-one on its domain. Justify your answer.`,pointName:`C1, C2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) For $x \\ne 2$, $f(x) = x + 2$, which has end behavior of a linear function. As $x \\to \\infty$, $f(x) \\to \\infty$. Notation: $\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} f(x) = \\infty$.
(ii) For $x \\ne 2$, $f(x) = x + 2$, which is strictly increasing on its domain. Therefore $f$ is one-to-one on its domain $\\{x : x \\ne 2\\}$.`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: correct limit notation with all four components ($\\lim$, $x \\to \\infty$, $f$ or $f(x)$, and $\\infty$). Point C2: correct conclusion (one-to-one) with valid justification (strictly increasing/decreasing or unique horizontal slope).`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`},{id:`frq-2a`,type:`modeling-non-periodic`,calcMode:`calc-required`,unitId:2,topicIds:[`2.5`,`2.13`,`2.14`],context:`A bacteria culture begins with 200 cells at time $t = 0$ hours. The population $P$ as a function of time $t$ in hours is modeled by $P(t) = 200 \\cdot e^{0.4t}$.`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`(i) Find $P(5)$ as a decimal approximation. Show the work that leads to your answer.
(ii) Find the value of $t$ when $P(t) = 1000$. Show your work.`,pointName:`A1, A2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) $P(5) = 200 \\cdot e^{0.4 \\cdot 5} = 200 \\cdot e^{2} = 200 \\cdot 7.389 \\approx 1477.811$.
(ii) Set $200 \\cdot e^{0.4t} = 1000 \\Rightarrow e^{0.4t} = 5 \\Rightarrow 0.4t = \\ln 5 \\Rightarrow t = \\frac{\\ln 5}{0.4} \\approx 4.024$ hours.`,scoringNotes:`Point A1: correct decimal 1477.811 with supporting work showing substitution. Point A2: correct value 4.024 with supporting work showing logarithm step.`},{letter:`B`,task:`(i) Find the average rate of change of $P$ on the interval $[0, 5]$.
(ii) Interpret the average rate of change in part B(i) using appropriate units.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) AROC $= \\frac{P(5) - P(0)}{5 - 0} = \\frac{1477.811 - 200}{5} = \\frac{1277.811}{5} \\approx 255.562$.
(ii) The bacteria population increases at an average rate of approximately 255.562 cells per hour over the first 5 hours.`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: correct decimal value 255.562. Point B2: interpretation that includes (1) increasing/positive rate, (2) units of cells per hour, (3) reference to time interval [0, 5].`},{letter:`C`,task:`A second bacteria culture is modeled by $Q(t) = 50 \\cdot 1.6^t$. Determine which culture, $P$ or $Q$, has a faster relative growth rate. Justify your answer.`,pointName:`C1, C2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`$P(t) = 200 \\cdot e^{0.4t}$. The continuous growth rate of $P$ is 0.4, so its annual growth factor is $e^{0.4} \\approx 1.492$.
$Q(t) = 50 \\cdot 1.6^t$. The hourly growth factor of $Q$ is 1.6.
Since $1.6 > 1.492$, culture $Q$ has the faster relative growth rate.`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: identifies a measure of growth rate (e.g., growth factor) for both cultures. Point C2: correct comparison with justification, concluding $Q$ has faster rate.`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`},{id:`frq-3a`,type:`modeling-periodic`,calcMode:`no-calc`,unitId:3,topicIds:[`3.5`,`3.6`,`3.7`],context:`The depth of water at the entrance of a harbor (in meters above the harbor floor) is modeled by a sinusoidal function. At $t = 0$ hours, the depth is at its minimum value of 5 meters. The depth then rises to a maximum of 15 meters and back to 5 meters every 12 hours.`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`Determine the amplitude, midline, and period of the sinusoidal function modeling the water depth.`,pointName:`A1, A2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`Amplitude = $(15 - 5)/2 = 5$ meters.
Midline: $(15 + 5)/2 = 10$ meters, so $y = 10$.
Period = 12 hours.`,scoringNotes:`Point A1: amplitude = 5 AND midline = 10 (or $y = 10$). Point A2: period = 12.`},{letter:`B`,task:`The depth $D$ as a function of time $t$ can be written in the form $D(t) = a \\cos(b(t - c)) + d$ or $D(t) = a \\sin(b(t - c)) + d$. Find values of $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$ for one such valid form.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`Use $D(t) = -5 \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6} t\\right) + 10$ (so $a = -5$, $b = \\pi/6$, $c = 0$, $d = 10$).
Verify: at $t = 0$, $D = -5 \\cdot 1 + 10 = 5$ (minimum). ✓ Period: $2\\pi / (\\pi/6) = 12$ hours. ✓`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: correct values of $a$ and $d$ (vertical transformations: amplitude/sign and midline). Point B2: correct values of $b$ and $c$ (horizontal transformations: period and phase).`,partialCreditNotes:`Multiple valid answers exist (e.g., using sine with a phase shift). Any valid combination earns the points.`},{letter:`C`,task:`For the model in part B, determine the depth at $t = 3$ hours and at $t = 9$ hours.`,pointName:`C1, C2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`At $t = 3$: $D(3) = -5 \\cos(\\pi/6 \\cdot 3) + 10 = -5 \\cos(\\pi/2) + 10 = -5(0) + 10 = 10$ meters.
At $t = 9$: $D(9) = -5 \\cos(\\pi/6 \\cdot 9) + 10 = -5 \\cos(3\\pi/2) + 10 = -5(0) + 10 = 10$ meters.`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: $D(3) = 10$ meters. Point C2: $D(9) = 10$ meters. Both must include units or context indication.`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`},{id:`frq-4a`,type:`symbolic-manipulations`,calcMode:`no-calc`,unitId:2,topicIds:[`2.4`,`2.12`,`2.13`],context:`Consider the equation $\\log_2(x + 1) + \\log_2(x - 1) = 3$.`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`Use properties of logarithms to rewrite the left-hand side as a single logarithm.`,pointName:`A1`,pointsAvailable:1,modelSolution:`By the product rule for logarithms: $\\log_2(x+1) + \\log_2(x-1) = \\log_2((x+1)(x-1)) = \\log_2(x^2 - 1)$.`,scoringNotes:`Point A1: correct combination using product rule. The simplified form $\\log_2(x^2 - 1)$ is required.`},{letter:`B`,task:`Solve the equation for $x$. Show all steps.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`$\\log_2(x^2 - 1) = 3$
$x^2 - 1 = 2^3 = 8$
$x^2 = 9$
$x = \\pm 3$
Check domain: need $x + 1 > 0$ AND $x - 1 > 0$, so $x > 1$. Reject $x = -3$. Therefore $x = 3$.`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: correctly converts to exponential form and solves $x^2 = 9$ to get $x = \\pm 3$. Point B2: correctly rejects $x = -3$ on domain grounds and reports $x = 3$ as the only solution.`},{letter:`C`,task:`Without using a calculator, determine the value of $\\log_8(x^2 - 1)$ when $x = 3$. Show your work.`,pointName:`C1`,pointsAvailable:1,modelSolution:`$x^2 - 1 = 9 - 1 = 8$. So $\\log_8(8) = 1$.`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: correct value 1 with supporting computation showing $x^2 - 1 = 8$.`},{letter:`D`,task:`Use a property of logarithms (other than the product rule) to rewrite $\\log_2(x^2 - 1)$ in an equivalent form.`,pointName:`D1, D2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`Method 1 (Power rule on factored form): $\\log_2((x-1)(x+1))$ — can be split (as in Part A) but that uses product rule again.
Method 2 (Change of base): $\\log_2(x^2 - 1) = \\dfrac{\\ln(x^2 - 1)}{\\ln 2}$.
Method 3 (Apply power rule to a factored equivalent): $\\log_2((x^2 - 1)) = \\log_2((x^2-1))$ — note that $x^2 - 1$ has no clean exponent, so power rule doesn't cleanly apply.
Accepted: change of base or rewriting as $\\log_2|x-1| + \\log_2|x+1|$ (treating with absolute values for general $x$).`,scoringNotes:`Point D1: chooses a valid log property (change of base, power rule, etc.) other than the product rule. Point D2: applies the property correctly without arithmetic errors.`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`},{id:`frq-1b`,type:`function-concepts`,calcMode:`calc-required`,unitId:1,topicIds:[`1.6`,`1.7`,`1.9`,`1.12`],context:`The function $f$ is given by $f(x) = \\dfrac{2x + 1}{x - 3}$. The function $g$ is given by $g(x) = (x + 1)^2 - 4$.`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`(i) Determine all vertical asymptotes of $f$, or indicate there are none.
(ii) Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f$, or indicate that there is none.`,pointName:`A1, A2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) Vertical asymptote where denominator is zero (and not canceled): $x - 3 = 0 \\Rightarrow x = 3$.
(ii) Numerator and denominator both have degree 1. Horizontal asymptote at $y = 2/1 = 2$.`,scoringNotes:`Point A1: $x = 3$. Point A2: $y = 2$.`},{letter:`B`,task:`(i) Find all values of $x$ for which $f(x) = 0$, or indicate there are none.
(ii) Find all values of $x$ for which $g(x) = 0$, or indicate there are none.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) $f(x) = 0$ when numerator $= 0$: $2x + 1 = 0 \\Rightarrow x = -1/2$. Verify $x = -1/2 \\ne 3$, so $x = -1/2$.
(ii) $g(x) = 0$: $(x+1)^2 = 4 \\Rightarrow x + 1 = \\pm 2 \\Rightarrow x = 1$ or $x = -3$.`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: $x = -1/2$. Point B2: $x = 1$ AND $x = -3$ (both required).`},{letter:`C`,task:`Determine all values of $x$ for which $f(x) = g(x)$, as decimal approximations. Show the work that leads to your answer.`,pointName:`C1, C2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`Set equal: $\\frac{2x+1}{x-3} = (x+1)^2 - 4 = x^2 + 2x - 3$.
Multiply both sides by $(x - 3)$: $2x + 1 = (x^2 + 2x - 3)(x - 3) = x^3 - x^2 - 9x + 9$.
So $x^3 - x^2 - 11x + 8 = 0$.
Using graphing calculator: $x \\approx -3.116$, $0.733$, $3.383$.
Verify $x \\ne 3$: all three are valid.`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: correctly sets up the equation $f(x) = g(x)$ and reduces to a polynomial equation. Point C2: three correct decimal solutions $\\approx -3.116$, $0.733$, $3.383$.`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`},{id:`frq-3b`,type:`modeling-periodic`,calcMode:`no-calc`,unitId:3,topicIds:[`3.5`,`3.7`,`3.10`],context:`A wheel of radius 2 meters rotates counterclockwise about its center at the origin, completing one rotation every 8 seconds. At $t = 0$, a point $P$ on the wheel is at position $(2, 0)$.`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`(i) Find the angular position $\\theta(t)$ of point $P$ in radians as a function of time $t$ in seconds.
(ii) Find $\\theta(2)$.`,pointName:`A1, A2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) Angular speed = $2\\pi / 8 = \\pi/4$ rad/sec. Starting at $\\theta = 0$: $\\theta(t) = (\\pi/4) t$.
(ii) $\\theta(2) = (\\pi/4)(2) = \\pi/2$.`,scoringNotes:`Point A1: $\\theta(t) = \\pi t / 4$. Point A2: $\\theta(2) = \\pi/2$.`},{letter:`B`,task:`Express the $x$- and $y$-coordinates of point $P$ as functions of $t$.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`$x(t) = r \\cos\\theta(t) = 2 \\cos(\\pi t / 4)$.
$y(t) = r \\sin\\theta(t) = 2 \\sin(\\pi t / 4)$.`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: correct $x(t)$. Point B2: correct $y(t)$.`},{letter:`C`,task:`Find all values of $t \\in [0, 8)$ for which the $y$-coordinate of $P$ equals $\\sqrt{2}$.`,pointName:`C1, C2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`$2 \\sin(\\pi t / 4) = \\sqrt{2} \\Rightarrow \\sin(\\pi t/4) = \\sqrt{2}/2$.
$\\pi t / 4 = \\pi/4$ or $\\pi t/4 = 3\\pi/4$ (since $\\sin = \\sqrt{2}/2$ at these in $[0, 2\\pi)$).
$t = 1$ or $t = 3$.
Check $[0, 8)$: both valid.`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: correct setup $\\sin(\\pi t/4) = \\sqrt{2}/2$. Point C2: $t = 1$ AND $t = 3$ (both required).`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`},{id:`frq-4b`,type:`symbolic-manipulations`,calcMode:`no-calc`,unitId:3,topicIds:[`3.10`,`3.12`],context:`Consider the equation $2 \\sin^2 \\theta + \\sin \\theta - 1 = 0$ for $\\theta \\in [0, 2\\pi)$.`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`Factor the left-hand side as a quadratic in $\\sin\\theta$.`,pointName:`A1`,pointsAvailable:1,modelSolution:`Treating $\\sin\\theta$ as the variable: $2u^2 + u - 1 = (2u - 1)(u + 1)$ where $u = \\sin\\theta$. So the equation becomes $(2\\sin\\theta - 1)(\\sin\\theta + 1) = 0$.`,scoringNotes:`Point A1: correct factored form $(2\\sin\\theta - 1)(\\sin\\theta + 1) = 0$ or equivalent.`},{letter:`B`,task:`Solve for all $\\theta \\in [0, 2\\pi)$. Show your work.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`From part A: $\\sin\\theta = 1/2$ OR $\\sin\\theta = -1$.
$\\sin\\theta = 1/2$: $\\theta = \\pi/6$ or $\\theta = 5\\pi/6$.
$\\sin\\theta = -1$: $\\theta = 3\\pi/2$.
Solutions: $\\theta = \\pi/6, 5\\pi/6, 3\\pi/2$.`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: correctly identifies both equations $\\sin\\theta = 1/2$ and $\\sin\\theta = -1$. Point B2: correct list of solutions $\\pi/6, 5\\pi/6, 3\\pi/2$.`},{letter:`C`,task:`Without solving the equation again, determine the number of solutions of $2 \\sin^2\\theta + \\sin\\theta - 1 = 0$ in the interval $[0, 4\\pi)$. Justify your answer.`,pointName:`C1, C2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`Each solution in $[0, 2\\pi)$ has a counterpart in $[2\\pi, 4\\pi)$ obtained by adding $2\\pi$ (since $\\sin$ has period $2\\pi$). There are 3 solutions in $[0, 2\\pi)$, so 6 solutions in $[0, 4\\pi)$.`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: correct count of 6. Point C2: justification using periodicity of sine.`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`},{id:`frq-2b`,type:`modeling-non-periodic`,calcMode:`calc-required`,unitId:1,topicIds:[`1.13`,`1.14`,`1.4`],context:`A company's monthly revenue (in thousands of dollars) for the first four months of operation is given in the table.`,contextSetup:`t (months) | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4
R(t) | 6 | 14 | 30 | 62`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`(i) Compute the successive ratios $R(t+1)/R(t)$ for $t = 1, 2, 3$.
(ii) Based on (i), explain whether a linear, quadratic, or exponential model is most appropriate for $R(t)$.`,pointName:`A1, A2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) $R(2)/R(1) = 14/6 \\approx 2.333$. $R(3)/R(2) = 30/14 \\approx 2.143$. $R(4)/R(3) = 62/30 \\approx 2.067$.
(ii) Ratios are not exactly constant — moving from 2.333 to 2.067 — but they are close-ish. However, FIRST differences are: 8, 16, 32 — exactly doubling, suggesting more complex structure. Looking at $R(t)$ values: $6, 14, 30, 62$. Notice $R(t) = 2^{t+1} + 2$ ? Check: $2^2 + 2 = 6 ✓$, $2^3 + 2 = 10$ ✗. Try $R(t) = 2^{t+1} - 2 \\cdot \\text{something}$... actually $R(t) = 4 \\cdot 2^t - 2$? $4 \\cdot 2 - 2 = 6 ✓$, $4 \\cdot 4 - 2 = 14 ✓$, $4 \\cdot 8 - 2 = 30 ✓$, $4 \\cdot 16 - 2 = 62 ✓$. So an exponential-plus-constant fits exactly: $R(t) = 4 \\cdot 2^t - 2$. Pure exponential is approximately appropriate.`,scoringNotes:`Point A1: three correct ratios. Point A2: identifies exponential as best model with reasoning based on near-constant ratios.`},{letter:`B`,task:`Construct an exponential model $R(t) = a \\cdot b^t + c$ that exactly fits the data, and identify the values of $a$, $b$, and $c$.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`From observation: $R(t) = 4 \\cdot 2^t - 2$.
Verify: $R(1) = 4(2) - 2 = 6 ✓$. $R(2) = 4(4) - 2 = 14 ✓$. $R(3) = 4(8) - 2 = 30 ✓$. $R(4) = 4(16) - 2 = 62 ✓$.
Values: $a = 4$, $b = 2$, $c = -2$.`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: identifies the form by recognizing pattern (e.g., differences double). Point B2: correct values $a = 4$, $b = 2$, $c = -2$ verified against data.`},{letter:`C`,task:`Based on your model from part B, predict the revenue at $t = 8$. State an assumption that limits the validity of this prediction.`,pointName:`C1, C2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(C1) $R(8) = 4 \\cdot 2^8 - 2 = 4 \\cdot 256 - 2 = 1022$ thousand dollars (i.e., $\\$1{,}022{,}000$).
(C2) Assumption: the company's exponential growth pattern must continue without external constraints (market saturation, competition, capacity limits). In practice, exponential growth cannot continue indefinitely, so this prediction may overstate actual revenue.`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: correct value 1022 (thousand) or equivalent. Point C2: any reasonable limiting assumption (market saturation, competition, capacity constraints, etc.).`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`},{id:`frq-1c`,type:`function-concepts`,calcMode:`calc-required`,unitId:1,topicIds:[`1.4`,`1.6`,`1.7`,`1.12`],context:`The function $h$ is given by $h(x) = (x - 1)^2 (x + 2)$. The function $k$ is given by $k(x) = h(x) + 5$.`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`(i) Determine all $x$-values where $h(x) = 0$.
(ii) Identify the multiplicity of each zero of $h$.`,pointName:`A1, A2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) $h(x) = 0 \\Rightarrow (x - 1)^2 (x + 2) = 0 \\Rightarrow x = 1$ or $x = -2$.
(ii) $x = 1$ has multiplicity 2; $x = -2$ has multiplicity 1.`,scoringNotes:`Point A1: $x = 1$ and $x = -2$ as zeros. Point A2: multiplicity 2 at $x = 1$ AND multiplicity 1 at $x = -2$.`},{letter:`B`,task:`(i) Find $h(0)$ and $k(0)$.
(ii) Express the end behavior of $h$ as $x \\to \\infty$ using mathematical limit notation.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) $h(0) = (0-1)^2(0+2) = (1)(2) = 2$. $k(0) = h(0) + 5 = 7$.
(ii) $h$ has degree 3 (odd) with positive leading coefficient. $\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} h(x) = \\infty$.`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: $h(0) = 2$ AND $k(0) = 7$. Point B2: limit notation with all four components ($\\lim$, $x \\to \\infty$, $h$ or $h(x)$, and $\\infty$).`},{letter:`C`,task:`(i) Find all values of $x$ for which $k(x) = 5$, as decimal approximations. Show the work that leads to your answer.
(ii) Determine whether $k$ is one-to-one on the interval $(0, 2)$. Justify your answer.`,pointName:`C1, C2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) $k(x) = 5 \\Rightarrow h(x) = 0 \\Rightarrow x = 1$ or $x = -2$. As decimals: $1.000$ and $-2.000$.
(ii) On $(0, 2)$, $h$ has a local minimum at $x = 1$ (because of the squared factor). So $h$ first decreases then increases on $(0, 2)$, meaning $h$ (and thus $k$) is NOT one-to-one on this interval.`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: $x = 1.000$ AND $x = -2.000$ with supporting work. Point C2: correctly states $k$ is NOT one-to-one with valid justification (e.g., local minimum at $x = 1$ within the interval, or specific counter-example showing two $x$-values mapped to the same output).`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`},{id:`frq-1d`,type:`function-concepts`,calcMode:`calc-required`,unitId:1,topicIds:[`1.7`,`1.8`,`1.9`,`1.10`,`2.7`],context:`The function $r$ is given by $r(x) = \\dfrac{x^2 - 5x + 6}{x^2 - 4}$. The function $s$ is given by $s(x) = x - 1$.`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`(i) Determine all zeros of $r$, or indicate there are none.
(ii) Determine all vertical asymptotes of $r$, or indicate there are none.`,pointName:`A1, A2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) Factor: $r(x) = \\dfrac{(x-2)(x-3)}{(x-2)(x+2)}$. Cancel $(x-2)$: $r(x) = \\dfrac{x-3}{x+2}$ for $x \\ne 2$. Zero at $x = 3$.
(ii) Vertical asymptote where SIMPLIFIED denominator is zero: $x + 2 = 0 \\Rightarrow x = -2$. (At $x = 2$ there is a hole, not a VA.)`,scoringNotes:`Point A1: $x = 3$ (and only $x = 3$). Point A2: $x = -2$ as the only VA.`},{letter:`B`,task:`(i) Identify the location of any holes in the graph of $r$, with both coordinates.
(ii) Find the horizontal asymptote of $r$.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) Hole at $x = 2$ (canceled factor). $y$-coordinate: simplified $r(2) = (2-3)/(2+2) = -1/4 = -0.250$. Hole at $(2, -0.250)$.
(ii) Both numerator and denominator have degree 2; HA at $y = $ ratio of leading coefficients $= 1/1 = 1$.`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: hole at $(2, -0.250)$ with both coordinates. Point B2: $y = 1$.`},{letter:`C`,task:`Find $(s \\circ r)(0)$ and state its decimal value to three decimal places.`,pointName:`C1, C2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(C1) $r(0) = (0 - 5(0) + 6)/(0 - 4) = 6/(-4) = -3/2 = -1.500$.
(C2) $(s \\circ r)(0) = s(r(0)) = s(-1.500) = -1.500 - 1 = -2.500$.`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: $r(0) = -1.500$ with supporting work. Point C2: $(s \\circ r)(0) = -2.500$.`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`},{id:`frq-1e`,type:`function-concepts`,calcMode:`calc-required`,unitId:1,topicIds:[`1.1`,`1.6`,`2.7`,`2.8`],context:`The graph of the function $f$, with domain $-3.5 \\le x \\le 3.5$, is shown. Three points on the graph are labeled. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = 2.916 \\cdot (0.7)^x$.`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`(i) Find the value of $h(3)$ where $h(x) = g(f(x))$. Show the work that leads to your answer.
(ii) Find all values of $x$ in the domain of $f$ for which $f(x) = 1$.`,pointName:`A1, A2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) From the graph, $f(3) = 1$ (a labeled point). So $h(3) = g(f(3)) = g(1) = 2.916 \\cdot (0.7)^1 = 2.916 \\cdot 0.7 = 2.041$.
(ii) From the graph, $f(x) = 1$ at the three labeled points: $x = -3, x = 0,$ and $x = 3$.`,scoringNotes:`Point A1: $h(3) \\approx 2.041$ (or 2.0412) with supporting work showing $f(3) = 1$ and $g(1)$ evaluation. Point A2: all three values $x = -3, 0, 3$.`},{letter:`B`,task:`(i) Find all values of $x$ for which $g(x) = 2$. Show the work that leads to your answer.
(ii) Find $\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} g(x)$, or indicate that it does not exist.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) Set $2.916 \\cdot (0.7)^x = 2 \\Rightarrow (0.7)^x = 2/2.916 \\approx 0.6859$. Take ln: $x \\ln(0.7) = \\ln(0.6859) \\Rightarrow x = \\ln(0.6859)/\\ln(0.7) \\approx -0.3771 / -0.3567 \\approx 1.057$.
(ii) $g(x) = 2.916 (0.7)^x$ with base $0.7 < 1$ ⇒ exponential decay. $\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} g(x) = 0$.`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: $x \\approx 1.057$ with supporting log work. Point B2: $\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} g(x) = 0$ in proper limit notation with all four components.`},{letter:`C`,task:`Determine whether $f$ has an inverse function. Provide a reason for your answer based on the graph.`,pointName:`C1, C2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`A function has an inverse if and only if it is one-to-one (passes the horizontal line test). The graph of $f$ shows three labeled points $(-3, 1), (0, 1),$ and $(3, 1)$ — three different inputs map to the same output of 1. Therefore $f$ is NOT one-to-one and does NOT have an inverse function on its domain $[-3.5, 3.5]$.`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: correctly states $f$ does NOT have an inverse. Point C2: valid reason citing the horizontal line test OR specifically pointing to the three labeled points sharing $y = 1$.`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`,graph:{id:`frq-1e-graph`,caption:`Graph of f on [−3.5, 3.5]. Three labeled points: (−3, 1), (0, 1), (3, 1).`,curves:[{fn:e=>1+(e*e*e-9*e)/9,strokeWidth:2,label:`y = f(x)`}],xDomain:[-3.5,3.5],yRange:[-2,4],points:[{x:-3,y:1,label:`(−3, 1)`,style:`star`},{x:0,y:1,label:`(0, 1)`,style:`star`},{x:3,y:1,label:`(3, 1)`,style:`star`}]}},{id:`frq-2c`,type:`modeling-non-periodic`,calcMode:`calc-required`,unitId:2,topicIds:[`2.5`,`2.13`,`2.14`],context:`A radioactive isotope has a half-life of 12 years. The amount $A$ (in grams) of the isotope present after $t$ years is modeled by $A(t) = 80 \\cdot (1/2)^{t/12}$.`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`(i) Find $A(0)$.
(ii) Find $A(24)$ as a decimal approximation.`,pointName:`A1, A2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) $A(0) = 80 \\cdot (1/2)^0 = 80 \\cdot 1 = 80$ grams.
(ii) $A(24) = 80 \\cdot (1/2)^{24/12} = 80 \\cdot (1/2)^2 = 80 \\cdot 0.25 = 20.000$ grams.`,scoringNotes:`Point A1: $A(0) = 80$ grams. Point A2: $A(24) = 20.000$ grams with supporting work.`},{letter:`B`,task:`(i) Find the time $t$, to three decimal places, at which $A(t) = 30$. Show your work.
(ii) Interpret the value found in (i) in the context of the model.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) $30 = 80 \\cdot (1/2)^{t/12}$. Divide: $(1/2)^{t/12} = 30/80 = 0.375$. Take $\\ln$: $(t/12) \\ln(0.5) = \\ln(0.375) \\Rightarrow t = 12 \\cdot \\ln(0.375)/\\ln(0.5) \\approx 12 \\cdot (-0.9808)/(-0.6931) \\approx 12 \\cdot 1.4150 \\approx 16.980$.
(ii) After approximately 16.980 years, the amount of isotope remaining is 30 grams.`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: $t \\approx 16.980$ years with supporting log work. Point B2: interpretation using context (time when amount is 30 grams) AND units of years.`},{letter:`C`,task:`Find the average rate of change of $A$ on the interval $[0, 12]$, to three decimal places. Interpret your result with appropriate units.`,pointName:`C1, C2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(C1) AROC $= (A(12) - A(0))/(12 - 0) = (40 - 80)/12 = -40/12 \\approx -3.333$.
(C2) The amount of isotope decreases at an average rate of approximately 3.333 grams per year over the first 12 years.`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: AROC $= -3.333$ (or equivalent, e.g., $-10/3$) with supporting work. Point C2: interpretation including (1) decreasing/negative direction, (2) units of grams per year, (3) reference to interval [0, 12].`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`},{id:`frq-2d`,type:`modeling-non-periodic`,calcMode:`calc-required`,unitId:2,topicIds:[`2.2`,`2.5`,`2.6`,`2.13`],context:`A scientist observes that the population $P$ of a bacteria culture (in thousands) at time $t$ (in hours) is recorded as: $P(0) = 5$, $P(1) = 7$, $P(2) = 9.8$, $P(3) = 13.72$.`,contextSetup:`t (hours) | 0 | 1 | 2 | 3
P(t) (thousands) | 5 | 7 | 9.8 | 13.72`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`(i) Compute the successive ratios $P(t+1)/P(t)$ for $t = 0, 1, 2$.
(ii) Based on the ratios, justify why an exponential model is appropriate for $P(t)$.`,pointName:`A1, A2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) $P(1)/P(0) = 7/5 = 1.400$. $P(2)/P(1) = 9.8/7 = 1.400$. $P(3)/P(2) = 13.72/9.8 = 1.400$.
(ii) The ratios are constant ($\\approx 1.400$). A constant ratio of successive outputs over equal input intervals is the defining feature of an exponential function. Therefore an exponential model is appropriate.`,scoringNotes:`Point A1: three correct ratios all equal to 1.400. Point A2: justifies exponential by referencing constant ratio property.`},{letter:`B`,task:`Construct an exponential model $P(t) = a \\cdot b^t$ that fits the data exactly. Identify $a$ and $b$.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`$P(0) = a = 5$.
From ratio: $b = 1.4$.
So $P(t) = 5 \\cdot (1.4)^t$.
Verify $P(3) = 5 \\cdot 1.4^3 = 5 \\cdot 2.744 = 13.72$ ✓.`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: $a = 5$ identified from $P(0)$. Point B2: $b = 1.4$ identified from ratio AND model verified against data.`},{letter:`C`,task:`(i) Use the model to find when $P(t) = 50$ (thousand), to three decimal places.
(ii) Express the model from part B in continuous form $P(t) = a \\cdot e^{kt}$. Identify $k$ to three decimal places.`,pointName:`C1, C2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) $50 = 5 \\cdot 1.4^t \\Rightarrow 1.4^t = 10 \\Rightarrow t \\ln 1.4 = \\ln 10 \\Rightarrow t = \\ln 10 / \\ln 1.4 \\approx 2.3026 / 0.3365 \\approx 6.842$ hours.
(ii) $b = e^k \\Rightarrow k = \\ln b = \\ln 1.4 \\approx 0.336$.`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: $t \\approx 6.842$ with supporting log work. Point C2: $k \\approx 0.336$ with $k = \\ln 1.4$ shown.`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`},{id:`frq-3c`,type:`modeling-periodic`,calcMode:`no-calc`,unitId:3,topicIds:[`3.5`,`3.7`,`3.10`],context:`A Ferris wheel has a radius of 10 meters and its center is 12 meters above the ground. The wheel rotates counterclockwise and completes one revolution every 4 minutes. At $t = 0$, a passenger is at the lowest point of the wheel.`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`Determine the amplitude, midline, and period of the sinusoidal function modeling the passenger's height $H(t)$ above the ground.`,pointName:`A1, A2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`Maximum height $= 12 + 10 = 22$ m. Minimum height $= 12 - 10 = 2$ m.
Amplitude $= (22 - 2)/2 = 10$ meters.
Midline $y = (22 + 2)/2 = 12$ meters.
Period $= 4$ minutes.`,scoringNotes:`Point A1: amplitude = 10 AND midline = 12. Point A2: period = 4.`},{letter:`B`,task:`Write a sinusoidal function $H(t) = a \\cos(b(t - c)) + d$ or $H(t) = a \\sin(b(t - c)) + d$ that models the passenger's height. Identify the values of $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`At $t = 0$, $H(0) = 2$ (minimum). Use $H(t) = -10 \\cos((\\pi/2) t) + 12$.
Values: $a = -10$, $b = \\pi/2$, $c = 0$, $d = 12$.
Verify: at $t = 0$, $H = -10 \\cdot 1 + 12 = 2$ ✓. Period $= 2\\pi / (\\pi/2) = 4$ ✓.`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: correct $a$ AND $d$ (vertical params: amplitude/sign and midline). Point B2: correct $b$ AND $c$ (horizontal params: period and phase).`,partialCreditNotes:`Multiple valid answers (e.g., $H(t) = 10 \\sin((\\pi/2)(t - 1)) + 12$ also works). Any valid combination earns the points.`},{letter:`C`,task:`(i) Find the height of the passenger at $t = 1$ minute.
(ii) Find all values of $t$ in $[0, 4)$ for which the height equals 17 meters.`,pointName:`C1, C2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) $H(1) = -10 \\cos((\\pi/2)(1)) + 12 = -10 \\cos(\\pi/2) + 12 = -10(0) + 12 = 12$ meters.
(ii) $-10 \\cos((\\pi/2) t) + 12 = 17 \\Rightarrow \\cos((\\pi/2) t) = -1/2$. So $(\\pi/2) t = 2\\pi/3$ or $(\\pi/2) t = 4\\pi/3$ (in $[0, 2\\pi)$). $t = 4/3$ or $t = 8/3$.`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: $H(1) = 12$ meters. Point C2: $t = 4/3$ AND $t = 8/3$ (both required).`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`},{id:`frq-3d`,type:`modeling-periodic`,calcMode:`no-calc`,unitId:3,topicIds:[`3.5`,`3.6`,`3.7`],context:`The number of daylight hours $L(d)$ at a certain northern city, where $d$ is the day number of the year (with $d = 1$ being January 1), can be modeled by a sinusoidal function. The maximum daylight is 16 hours on $d = 172$ (June 21), and the minimum is 8 hours on $d = 355$ (December 21).`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`Determine the amplitude, midline, and period of $L(d)$.`,pointName:`A1, A2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`Amplitude $= (16 - 8)/2 = 4$ hours.
Midline $y = (16 + 8)/2 = 12$ hours.
Period $= 365$ days.`,scoringNotes:`Point A1: amplitude = 4 AND midline = 12. Point A2: period = 365.`},{letter:`B`,task:`Write a sinusoidal function $L(d) = a \\cos(b(d - c)) + d$ with the maximum at $d = 172$. Identify $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`$L(d) = 4 \\cos\\!\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{365}(d - 172)\\right) + 12$.
Values: $a = 4$, $b = 2\\pi/365$, $c = 172$, $d = 12$.
Verify: at $d = 172$, $\\cos 0 = 1$, $L = 4 + 12 = 16$ ✓.`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: correct $a = 4$ AND $d = 12$. Point B2: correct $b = 2\\pi/365$ AND $c = 172$.`},{letter:`C`,task:`(i) On the spring equinox ($d \\approx 80$, around March 21), the daylight is approximately equal to the midline value. Use the model to confirm or refute this claim.
(ii) Determine, in terms of the model, on what day numbers the daylight is exactly 12 hours.`,pointName:`C1, C2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) $L(80) = 4 \\cos\\!\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{365}(80 - 172)\\right) + 12 = 4 \\cos\\!\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi (-92)}{365}\\right) + 12$. Inner argument: $(-92)/(365/2) = -92/182.5 \\approx -0.504$, multiplied by $\\pi$: $\\approx -1.585$ rad (close to $-\\pi/2$). $\\cos(-\\pi/2) = 0$, so $L(80) \\approx 12$ — confirming the claim.
(ii) $L(d) = 12 \\Rightarrow \\cos\\!\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{365}(d - 172)\\right) = 0$. So $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{365}(d - 172) = \\pm\\pi/2 + k \\cdot 2\\pi$. Solving: $d - 172 = \\pm 365/4 + k \\cdot 365 = \\pm 91.25 + 365 k$. For $d \\in [1, 365]$: $d = 172 - 91.25 = 80.75$ (spring) or $d = 172 + 91.25 = 263.25$ (autumn). I.e., approximately $d = 80$ and $d = 263$.`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: correctly evaluates $L(80) \\approx 12$, confirming claim. Point C2: identifies both equinox days (approximately $d = 80$ and $d = 263$) using $\\cos = 0$ argument.`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`},{id:`frq-4c`,type:`symbolic-manipulations`,calcMode:`no-calc`,unitId:1,topicIds:[`1.11`,`1.7`],context:`Consider the rational expression $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^3 - 1}{x^2 - 1}$.`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`Factor both the numerator and the denominator of $f(x)$ completely over the real numbers.`,pointName:`A1, A2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`Numerator: $x^3 - 1 = (x - 1)(x^2 + x + 1)$ (difference of cubes).
Denominator: $x^2 - 1 = (x - 1)(x + 1)$ (difference of squares).`,scoringNotes:`Point A1: correct factorization of numerator using difference of cubes. Point A2: correct factorization of denominator using difference of squares.`},{letter:`B`,task:`Simplify $f(x)$, indicating any restrictions on $x$.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`$f(x) = \\dfrac{(x - 1)(x^2 + x + 1)}{(x - 1)(x + 1)} = \\dfrac{x^2 + x + 1}{x + 1}$ for $x \\ne 1$.
Additional restriction: $x \\ne -1$ (denominator zero in original).
Final: $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^2 + x + 1}{x + 1}$ for $x \\ne 1$ and $x \\ne -1$.`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: correct simplification $\\dfrac{x^2 + x + 1}{x + 1}$. Point B2: notes both restrictions $x \\ne 1$ and $x \\ne -1$.`},{letter:`C`,task:`(i) Identify the location of any holes in the graph of $f(x)$, with both coordinates.
(ii) Identify all vertical asymptotes of $f(x)$.`,pointName:`C1, C2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) Hole at $x = 1$ (canceled factor). $y$-coordinate: simplified $f(1) = (1 + 1 + 1)/(1 + 1) = 3/2$. Hole at $(1, 3/2)$.
(ii) Vertical asymptote where simplified denominator is zero: $x + 1 = 0 \\Rightarrow x = -1$.`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: hole at $(1, 3/2)$ with both coordinates. Point C2: $x = -1$ as the only VA.`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`},{id:`frq-3e`,type:`modeling-periodic`,calcMode:`no-calc`,unitId:3,topicIds:[`3.5`,`3.6`,`3.7`],context:`A Ferris wheel rotates at a constant rate. The height $h$, in meters, of a passenger above the ground at time $t$, in minutes, is modeled by a sinusoidal function. The graph of $h$ is shown for two complete cycles. The dashed line indicates the midline. Five points labeled F, G, J, K, and P are evenly spaced at quarter-period intervals along one full cycle starting at the maximum.

The Ferris wheel completes one revolution every $4$ minutes. The maximum height of a passenger is $22$ meters, and the minimum height is $2$ meters.

No scale is indicated on the axes.`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`(i) Provide possible coordinates $(t, h(t))$ for each of the five labeled points F, G, J, K, and P, given the order F (maximum), G (descending midline crossing), J (minimum), K (ascending midline crossing), P (next maximum).
(ii) Verify that your coordinates are spaced at quarter-period intervals.`,pointName:`A1, A2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) Period $= 4$ min, so quarter-period $= 1$ min. Amplitude $= (22 - 2)/2 = 10$. Midline $= (22 + 2)/2 = 12$.
Possible coordinates (taking F at $t = 0$):
F $(0, 22)$ — maximum
G $(1, 12)$ — descending midline
J $(2, 2)$ — minimum
K $(3, 12)$ — ascending midline
P $(4, 22)$ — next maximum.
(ii) $\\Delta t = 1$ between each consecutive pair, equal to one-quarter of the period $4/4 = 1$. ✓`,scoringNotes:`Point A1: correct $h(t)$-coordinates: F and P at 22, G and K at 12, J at 2 (height of midline AND amplitude correctly applied). Point A2: correct $t$-coordinates spaced at quarter-period intervals (any horizontal shift is acceptable as long as $\\Delta t = 1$ between consecutive labeled points).`,partialCreditNotes:`Negative t-values and any starting offset are accepted as long as quarter-period spacing is preserved.`},{letter:`B`,task:`The function $h$ can be written in the form $h(t) = a \\sin(b(t + c)) + d$ or $h(t) = a \\cos(b(t + c)) + d$. Find values of $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$ for one valid form.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`Using the F at $t = 0$ (maximum): cosine form is natural.
$h(t) = 10 \\cos\\!\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} t\\right) + 12$ (with $a = 10$, $b = \\pi/2$, $c = 0$, $d = 12$).
Verify: $h(0) = 10 \\cdot 1 + 12 = 22$ ✓ (maximum). Period: $2\\pi / (\\pi/2) = 4$ ✓.
Alternate (sine form): $h(t) = 10 \\sin\\!\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}(t + 1)\\right) + 12$.`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: correct $a$ AND $d$ (vertical params: amplitude and midline). Point B2: correct $b$ AND $c$ (horizontal params: angular frequency and phase). Multiple equivalent forms accepted.`},{letter:`C`,task:`Let $t_1$ and $t_2$ denote the $t$-coordinates of points G and J respectively (so $t_1 < t_2$ and $h(t_1)$ is the midline value, $h(t_2)$ is the minimum). On the open interval $(t_1, t_2)$, describe both (i) whether $h$ is positive or negative AND increasing or decreasing; (ii) the concavity of $h$ AND whether the rate of change is increasing or decreasing.`,pointName:`C1, C2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) On $(t_1, t_2) = (1, 2)$: $h$ goes from midline $(12)$ DOWN to minimum $(2)$. So $h$ is POSITIVE (since both midline and min are positive) AND DECREASING.
(ii) Coming down from midline to a minimum: the curve is concave UP (curving back up toward eventual rise). Equivalently, the rate of change is INCREASING (becoming less negative as it approaches the minimum where rate $= 0$).`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: correctly identifies $h$ as positive AND decreasing on $(t_1, t_2)$. Point C2: correctly identifies concave up AND rate of change increasing (with valid justification linking the two — at a minimum, rate transitions from negative to positive, so on the interval ending at the minimum, rate must be increasing).`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`,graph:{id:`frq-3e-graph`,caption:`Schematic graph of h(t). Period and amplitude given in prose. No scale on axes.`,curves:[{fn:e=>10*Math.cos(Math.PI/2*e)+12,strokeWidth:2}],xDomain:[-.5,8.5],yRange:[-2,26],schematic:!0,xLabel:`t`,yLabel:`h(t)`,asymptotes:[{type:`horizontal`,value:12,label:`midline`}],points:[{x:0,y:22,label:`F`,style:`star`},{x:1,y:12,label:`G`,style:`star`},{x:2,y:2,label:`J`,style:`star`},{x:3,y:12,label:`K`,style:`star`},{x:4,y:22,label:`P`,style:`star`}]}},{id:`frq-3f`,type:`modeling-periodic`,calcMode:`no-calc`,unitId:3,topicIds:[`3.5`,`3.6`,`3.7`],context:`A point on a vibrating guitar string oscillates vertically with simple harmonic motion. The displacement $d$, in millimeters, of the point above its rest position at time $t$, in seconds, is modeled by a sinusoidal function. The graph of $d$ is shown for two complete cycles. The dashed line indicates the midline (rest position). Five points labeled F, G, J, K, and P are evenly spaced at quarter-period intervals along one full cycle starting at the ascending midline crossing.

The string vibrates at a frequency of $200$ cycles per second (so the period is $1/200$ seconds). The amplitude of vibration is $4$ millimeters.

No scale is indicated on the axes.`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`(i) Provide possible coordinates $(t, d(t))$ for each of the five labeled points F, G, J, K, and P, given the order F (ascending midline), G (maximum), J (descending midline), K (minimum), P (next ascending midline).
(ii) Verify that your coordinates are spaced at quarter-period intervals.`,pointName:`A1, A2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) Period $= 1/200 = 0.005$ s. Quarter-period $= 1/800 = 0.00125$ s. Amplitude $= 4$ mm. Midline $= 0$ (rest position).
Possible coordinates (taking F at $t = 0$):
F $(0, 0)$ — ascending midline
G $(1/800, 4)$ — maximum
J $(2/800, 0)$ — descending midline
K $(3/800, -4)$ — minimum
P $(4/800 = 1/200, 0)$ — next ascending midline.
(ii) $\\Delta t = 1/800$ between each consecutive pair, equal to one-quarter of the period $1/200 \\div 4 = 1/800$. ✓`,scoringNotes:`Point A1: correct $d(t)$-coordinates: F, J, P at 0, G at 4, K at $-4$. Point A2: correct $t$-coordinates spaced by $1/800$ (any consistent shift accepted).`},{letter:`B`,task:`The function $d$ can be written in the form $d(t) = a \\sin(b(t + c)) + e$ or $d(t) = a \\cos(b(t + c)) + e$. Find values of $a$, $b$, $c$, and $e$ for one valid form.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`Using F at $t = 0$ (ascending midline): pure sine form.
$d(t) = 4 \\sin(400 \\pi t) + 0$ (with $a = 4$, $b = 400 \\pi$, $c = 0$, $e = 0$).
Verify: at $t = 0$, $d = 4 \\sin 0 = 0$ ✓. At $t = 1/800$, $d = 4 \\sin(400\\pi / 800) = 4 \\sin(\\pi/2) = 4$ ✓. Period: $2\\pi / 400\\pi = 1/200$ ✓.`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: correct $a = 4$ AND $e = 0$. Point B2: correct $b = 400\\pi$ (or $2\\pi/(\\text{period}) = 2\\pi / (1/200)$) AND $c$ consistent with chosen form.`},{letter:`C`,task:`Let $t_1$ and $t_2$ denote the $t$-coordinates of points G and J respectively. On the open interval $(t_1, t_2)$, describe both (i) whether $d$ is increasing or decreasing AND positive or negative; (ii) the concavity of $d$ AND whether the rate of change is increasing or decreasing.`,pointName:`C1, C2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) On $(t_1, t_2) = (1/800, 2/800)$: $d$ goes from maximum $(4)$ DOWN to descending midline $(0)$. So $d$ is POSITIVE (above rest position) AND DECREASING.
(ii) Coming down from a maximum, the curve is concave DOWN (inflection at the midline). Equivalently, the rate of change is DECREASING (becoming more negative).`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: $d$ positive AND decreasing on $(t_1, t_2)$. Point C2: concave down AND rate of change decreasing.`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`,graph:{id:`frq-3f-graph`,caption:`Schematic graph of d(t). Period 1/200 s and amplitude 4 mm given in prose. No scale on axes.`,curves:[{fn:e=>4*Math.sin(400*Math.PI*e),strokeWidth:2}],xDomain:[-5e-4,.0105],yRange:[-6,6],schematic:!0,xLabel:`t`,yLabel:`d(t)`,asymptotes:[{type:`horizontal`,value:0,label:`midline (rest)`}],points:[{x:0,y:0,label:`F`,style:`star`},{x:1/800,y:4,label:`G`,style:`star`},{x:2/800,y:0,label:`J`,style:`star`},{x:3/800,y:-4,label:`K`,style:`star`},{x:4/800,y:0,label:`P`,style:`star`}]}},{id:`frq-1f`,type:`function-concepts`,calcMode:`calc-required`,unitId:1,topicIds:[`1.4`,`1.7`,`2.7`,`2.8`],context:`The function $p$ is defined by $p(x) = (x - 4)(x + 1)^2$. The function $q$ is defined by $q(x) = \\dfrac{p(x)}{x - 4}$.`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`(i) Determine all real zeros of $p$, with their multiplicities.
(ii) Determine the end behavior of $p$ in limit notation.`,pointName:`A1, A2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) $p(x) = 0$ at $x = 4$ (multiplicity 1) and $x = -1$ (multiplicity 2).
(ii) Degree 3 polynomial with positive leading coefficient: $\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} p(x) = \\infty$ and $\\lim_{x \\to -\\infty} p(x) = -\\infty$.`,scoringNotes:`Point A1: zeros and multiplicities both correct. Point A2: both limits in proper notation.`},{letter:`B`,task:`(i) Simplify $q(x)$ and state any restriction on $x$.
(ii) Identify any holes in the graph of $q$ with both coordinates.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) $q(x) = \\dfrac{(x - 4)(x + 1)^2}{x - 4} = (x + 1)^2$ for $x \\ne 4$.
(ii) Hole at $x = 4$: $y = (4 + 1)^2 = 25$. Hole at $(4, 25)$.`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: simplified $(x+1)^2$ AND restriction $x \\ne 4$. Point B2: hole at $(4, 25)$ with both coordinates.`},{letter:`C`,task:`Use a calculator to find the average rate of change of $q$ on the interval $[0, 3]$, to three decimal places. Interpret the units in context (assume $x$ is in seconds and $q$ is in meters).`,pointName:`C1, C2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(C1) $q(0) = (0+1)^2 = 1$. $q(3) = (3+1)^2 = 16$. AROC $= (16 - 1)/(3 - 0) = 15/3 = 5.000$.
(C2) The quantity $q$ increases at an average rate of 5 meters per second over the interval from $t = 0$ to $t = 3$ seconds.`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: AROC = 5.000 with shown computation. Point C2: includes positive direction, units (m/s), and reference to interval.`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`},{id:`frq-2e`,type:`modeling-non-periodic`,calcMode:`calc-required`,unitId:2,topicIds:[`2.5`,`2.13`,`2.14`],context:`A drug administered to a patient is eliminated according to $C(t) = 50 e^{-0.231 t}$, where $C$ is the concentration in mg/L and $t$ is hours after administration.`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`(i) State the initial concentration $C(0)$.
(ii) Find $C(4)$ to three decimal places.`,pointName:`A1, A2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) $C(0) = 50 e^0 = 50$ mg/L.
(ii) $C(4) = 50 e^{-0.924} \\approx 50 \\cdot 0.3970 \\approx 19.849$ mg/L.`,scoringNotes:`Point A1: $C(0) = 50$. Point A2: $C(4) \\approx 19.849$ with supporting work.`},{letter:`B`,task:`(i) Find the half-life of the drug, to three decimal places.
(ii) Express $C(t)$ in the form $C(t) = 50 \\cdot b^{t}$, identifying $b$ to four decimal places.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) Half-life $t_{1/2}$: $50 e^{-0.231 t} = 25 \\Rightarrow e^{-0.231 t} = 0.5 \\Rightarrow -0.231 t = \\ln 0.5 \\Rightarrow t = \\ln 2 / 0.231 \\approx 3.001$ hours.
(ii) $b = e^{-0.231} \\approx 0.7938$. So $C(t) = 50 \\cdot (0.7938)^t$.`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: $t_{1/2} \\approx 3.001$ with log work shown. Point B2: $b \\approx 0.7938$.`},{letter:`C`,task:`Determine the time, to three decimal places, at which the concentration is 1 mg/L. Interpret your answer in the context of the problem.`,pointName:`C1, C2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(C1) $50 e^{-0.231 t} = 1 \\Rightarrow e^{-0.231 t} = 0.02 \\Rightarrow t = \\ln 0.02 / (-0.231) \\approx -3.912 / -0.231 \\approx 16.937$ hours.
(C2) After approximately 16.937 hours, the drug concentration drops to 1 mg/L.`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: $t \\approx 16.937$ with log work. Point C2: interpretation includes time and concentration with units.`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`},{id:`frq-3g`,type:`modeling-periodic`,calcMode:`no-calc`,unitId:3,topicIds:[`3.5`,`3.6`,`3.7`],context:`A pendulum swings back and forth. The horizontal displacement (in cm) of the pendulum bob from its rest position at time $t$ (in seconds) is modeled by $x(t) = 8 \\cos(\\pi t)$.`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`(i) State the amplitude and period of $x$.
(ii) Find $x(0)$ and $x(0.5)$.`,pointName:`A1, A2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`(i) Amplitude $= 8$ cm. Period $= 2\\pi/\\pi = 2$ seconds.
(ii) $x(0) = 8 \\cos 0 = 8$ cm. $x(0.5) = 8 \\cos(\\pi/2) = 0$ cm.`,scoringNotes:`Point A1: amplitude AND period correct. Point A2: both function values correct.`},{letter:`B`,task:`Find all values of $t \\in [0, 4)$ for which $x(t) = -4$.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`$8 \\cos(\\pi t) = -4 \\Rightarrow \\cos(\\pi t) = -1/2$.
In $[0, 2\\pi)$: $\\pi t = 2\\pi/3$ or $\\pi t = 4\\pi/3$. So $t = 2/3$ or $t = 4/3$.
Using periodicity (period 2 s), in $[2, 4)$: add 2 to get $t = 8/3$ and $t = 10/3$.
Solutions: $t = 2/3, 4/3, 8/3, 10/3$.`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: correctly reduces to $\\cos(\\pi t) = -1/2$ and finds $t = 2/3, 4/3$ in first period. Point B2: extends to second period correctly.`},{letter:`C`,task:`On the open interval $(0, 0.5)$, describe the direction (increasing/decreasing) AND concavity of $x$. Justify briefly using values from part A and the shape of cosine.`,pointName:`C1, C2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`On $(0, 0.5)$, $x$ goes from $8$ down to $0$, so $x$ is DECREASING. From the maximum at $t = 0$ down to the midline crossing at $t = 0.5$, the curve is concave DOWN (rate of change is becoming more negative — the bob accelerates downward through the midline).`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: identifies decreasing on $(0, 0.5)$. Point C2: concave down with valid justification.`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`},{id:`frq-4e`,type:`symbolic-manipulations`,calcMode:`no-calc`,unitId:2,topicIds:[`2.4`,`2.12`,`2.13`],context:`Consider the equation $4^x - 2^{x+1} - 8 = 0$.`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`Rewrite the equation as a quadratic in $u = 2^x$.`,pointName:`A1`,pointsAvailable:1,modelSolution:`Let $u = 2^x$. Then $4^x = (2^x)^2 = u^2$ and $2^{x+1} = 2 \\cdot 2^x = 2u$.
The equation becomes $u^2 - 2u - 8 = 0$.`,scoringNotes:`Point A1: correct quadratic $u^2 - 2u - 8 = 0$ with the substitution $u = 2^x$ shown.`},{letter:`B`,task:`Solve the quadratic for $u$, then for $x$. Reject any extraneous solutions.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`Factor: $u^2 - 2u - 8 = (u - 4)(u + 2) = 0$. So $u = 4$ or $u = -2$.
Since $u = 2^x > 0$, reject $u = -2$.
$2^x = 4 = 2^2 \\Rightarrow x = 2$.`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: factors correctly and finds both roots. Point B2: rejects negative root and reports $x = 2$.`},{letter:`C`,task:`Verify the solution by substituting back into the original equation.`,pointName:`C1`,pointsAvailable:1,modelSolution:`At $x = 2$: $4^2 - 2^{3} - 8 = 16 - 8 - 8 = 0$. ✓ Solution verified.`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: substitution shown numerically.`},{letter:`D`,task:`Use change-of-base to express $x = 2$ as $\\log_2 4$ AND as $\\ln 4 / \\ln 2$.`,pointName:`D1, D2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`$x = \\log_2 4 = 2$ since $2^2 = 4$.
Also $x = \\ln 4 / \\ln 2 = 2 \\ln 2 / \\ln 2 = 2$.`,scoringNotes:`Point D1: $\\log_2 4 = 2$ correctly. Point D2: $\\ln 4 / \\ln 2$ correctly simplified to 2.`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`},{id:`frq-4d`,type:`symbolic-manipulations`,calcMode:`no-calc`,unitId:3,topicIds:[`3.10`,`3.12`],context:`Consider the equation $\\cos(2\\theta) + \\cos\\theta = 0$ for $\\theta \\in [0, 2\\pi)$.`,parts:[{letter:`A`,task:`Use a double-angle identity to rewrite the equation in terms of $\\cos\\theta$ only.`,pointName:`A1`,pointsAvailable:1,modelSolution:`Use $\\cos(2\\theta) = 2\\cos^2\\theta - 1$. Substitute: $2\\cos^2\\theta - 1 + \\cos\\theta = 0$, i.e., $2\\cos^2\\theta + \\cos\\theta - 1 = 0$.`,scoringNotes:`Point A1: correct rewrite $2\\cos^2\\theta + \\cos\\theta - 1 = 0$ using a valid double-angle form.`},{letter:`B`,task:`Solve the rewritten equation for $\\cos\\theta$. Show factoring or other algebraic steps.`,pointName:`B1, B2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`Treating $u = \\cos\\theta$: $2u^2 + u - 1 = (2u - 1)(u + 1) = 0$.
So $u = 1/2$ or $u = -1$.
Meaning: $\\cos\\theta = 1/2$ or $\\cos\\theta = -1$.`,scoringNotes:`Point B1: correct factorization $(2\\cos\\theta - 1)(\\cos\\theta + 1) = 0$. Point B2: identifies both $\\cos\\theta = 1/2$ AND $\\cos\\theta = -1$.`},{letter:`C`,task:`Solve for all $\\theta \\in [0, 2\\pi)$. Express each solution exactly.`,pointName:`C1, C2`,pointsAvailable:2,modelSolution:`$\\cos\\theta = 1/2$: $\\theta = \\pi/3$ or $\\theta = 5\\pi/3$.
$\\cos\\theta = -1$: $\\theta = \\pi$.
All solutions: $\\theta = \\pi/3, \\pi, 5\\pi/3$.`,scoringNotes:`Point C1: solutions to $\\cos\\theta = 1/2$ correctly identified ($\\pi/3$ and $5\\pi/3$). Point C2: $\\theta = \\pi$ included; final list correct.`},{letter:`D`,task:`Without solving the equation again, determine the number of solutions in $[0, 4\\pi)$. Justify.`,pointName:`D1`,pointsAvailable:1,modelSolution:`Each solution in $[0, 2\\pi)$ has a counterpart in $[2\\pi, 4\\pi)$ obtained by adding $2\\pi$ (period of cosine is $2\\pi$). Three solutions in $[0, 2\\pi)$ implies six solutions in $[0, 4\\pi)$.`,scoringNotes:`Point D1: count of 6 with valid justification using periodicity.`}],totalPoints:6,source:`precalc-original`}];

